# AGENTS.md — context for AI coding agents

> Read this file at the start of every session. It points to the source of truth for project rules and workflow.
> Universal format read by Claude Code (CLAUDE.md fallback), Codex CLI, Cursor, Windsurf, Roo Code, and others.
>
> **Authoritative for:** AI-agent session-context format and pointer structure for consumer projects; which install depth is present and where the authoritative criteria live.
> **NOT authoritative for:** project goal — see consumer's README.md. Rule text — see `.ai-factory/RULES.md`. Tier criteria + degradation matrix — see `.ai-factory/tier-home.md`. Lifecycle past install — see `.ai-factory/AI-USAGE-GUIDE.md`.

**This file is a POINTER DOC.** It is the thin always-on layer; everything below is one line of orientation plus the path that owns the detail. If you find the full answer here rather than in the file named, that is drift — the named file wins.

## What this project is

See `.ai-factory/DESCRIPTION.md` for full project context — domain, stack, constraints, non-goals.
See `.ai-factory/ARCHITECTURE.md` for layer structure, dependency direction, forbidden patterns — and on a React/Next project also `.ai-factory/ARCHITECTURE.react-next.md` (Server vs Client boundary).

## Project rules (enforced by lint, tests, CI, audit-ai-docs)

`.ai-factory/RULES.md` is the rule list and the only place rules are stated — R1–R11 for every stack, plus R12–R20 for React/Next (`.ai-factory/RULES.react-next.md`) and IR1–IR6 for microservices (`.ai-factory/rules/integration-rules.md`). Each rule names its own enforcement channel there. Read it before your first edit; do not rely on a summary.

**Runtime-discipline rules R7/R8 are opt-in.** `no-direct-time-randomness` (R7) and `require-otel-span` (R8) need an injected Clock/Random + `infrastructure/` layer and an OpenTelemetry tracer — infra a fresh skeleton lacks. The shipped `eslint.config.mjs` enables them only when `AIF_STRICT_RUNTIME=1` is set; R2 (validation at boundaries) stays unconditional.

## Environment layer — which depth is installed here

The framework installs at one of three monotonic depths. **No flag is recorded on disk, so detect the depth by what is present** — then read only the row that matches:

| Detect (from the project root)                                            | Depth     | What the environment layer gives you here                                                                                                                                                        |
| ------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `scripts/audit-ai-docs.sh` present, `.ai-factory/tier-home.md` **absent** | `core`    | Rules + tests + gate hooks + CI. No model-tier routing surface is installed, so tier-routing instructions do not apply to this project.                                                          |
| `.ai-factory/tier-home.md` **and** `.claude/skills/arch/` present         | `env`     | core + the multi-model contour surface: tier-routing criteria on disk, and the `/arch` design contour.                                                                                           |
| also `.claude/skills/pipeline/` present                                   | `factory` | env + the 7-skill operator suite (`pipeline`, `dispatcher`, `harvest`, `aif-doctor`, `night-mode`, `story`, `claude-glm-executor-handoff`). Presupposes the aif-handoff runtime on this machine. |

**Criteria and degradations live in ONE file, not here.** When work needs model-tier routing (Tier 0 tiny / Tier 1 bulky-simple / Tier 2 bulky-complex), or when a capability is absent (no aif runtime, no executor-tier model subscription, no top-tier advisor model, non-Claude-Code harness), the authoritative criteria **and** the per-capability degradation matrix are `.ai-factory/tier-home.md` (§2 criteria, §3 degradations). This file states no criterion and no degradation row — open that file. On a `core` install it is absent by design: nothing above the lint / hook / CI layers was installed, so there is no tier system to degrade.

**Lifecycle past install** — `.ai-factory/AI-USAGE-GUIDE.md`: First Steps for each depth, the daily cycle, harness portability, and what is not shipped yet (named with an owner, not described as if it existed).

## Workflow

### Gate before commit / PR (always present — shipped by this installer)

Before every commit or PR, run the gate this installer actually ships:

- **`./scripts/audit-ai-docs.sh`** — drift + code-vs-docs probes (~10 sec). Run it before you commit.
- **`./scripts/check-rule-globs.sh`** — fails if a custom ESLint rule (R2 always; R7/R8 under `AIF_STRICT_RUNTIME=1`) matches **zero** source files, i.e. is silently inert against your layout. Wired into `npm run validate` and the shipped CI `lint` job. Widening the globs, monorepo shadowing-package FAIL/WARN semantics, and the brownfield-CI case where the gate runs local-only: `.ai-factory/AI-USAGE-GUIDE.md` §3.1.
- **`./scripts/check-lintstaged-resolves.sh`** — fails if a `.lintstagedrc.json` command's binary can't resolve from the directory lint-staged would run it in. Run after `npm/pnpm install`. Monorepo `ENOENT`-on-commit case and its per-package fix: `.ai-factory/AI-USAGE-GUIDE.md` §3.1.
- **`./scripts/check-fences-fire.sh`** — plants deliberately-bad input and asserts the installed rules go RED on it. An installed rule never seen to fire is an unproven claim.
- The **pre-push hook** (`.husky/pre-push`) fires automatically on `git push` — typecheck, `vitest related`, dependency-cruiser.
- **CI** gates the PR (`ci-success` required check). This is the last-resort authority that does not depend on local tooling.

These are unconditional — they ship with the project and need no extra install.

### Structural code search (ast-grep)

For structure-aware queries — counting real array/object members, finding language constructs, or any search where plain-text grep can false-positive on comments, strings, or code fences — prefer `ast-grep` over regex grep: `ast-grep run --pattern '<pattern>' --lang <lang> .` (install if missing: `npm install -g @ast-grep/cli`). Plain grep stays fine for simple string lookups.

### Forbidden

- `git commit --no-verify` — bypassing pre-commit/pre-push hooks.
- `git push -f` to `main`.
- Skipping `./scripts/audit-ai-docs.sh` / the pre-push hook before PR.
- Adding rules to AGENTS.md / RULES.md without a measurable check.

