## Rule research — continue post-install and on demand

The framework's thesis is that rules are tested for firing at delivery time, not shipped pre-baked. The installer delivered a curated starter set; researching stack-specific rules from live documentation is the next step of the same lifecycle.

- **First invocation point:** the agent that just installed the framework continues directly into `/rule-research` in the same session, before reporting back to the operator. Research is skipped **only** on an explicit operator opt-out — surface the opt-out at first contact, do NOT silently assume one.
- **On demand (any session):** re-invoke `/rule-research` to add or refresh a stack-specific rule. `.claude/agents/rule-researcher.md` holds the per-stack author→render→join→lock sequence and the honest per-stack limits (a lane whose support is partial says so there).

## Skills available

These skills are installed under `.claude/skills/` and **auto-activate on relevant queries in Claude Code**:

- **`getff`** — five-layer framework (architecture / meta-tests / spec by example / mutation / living docs), plus 5 reference docs in `references/`. On Claude Code, auto-triggers on questions about ESLint, Stryker, Pact, dependency-cruiser, AI-driven code drift.
- **`tool-bootstrapping`** — when project deps change, analyses the stack for MCP/skill fit, proposes a confirmation list, persists accepted/rejected decisions in `.ai-factory/tool-decisions.md`.
- **`rule-research`** / **`rule-tests`** — bootstrap rules from live documentation; write or repair the firing test material that proves a rule fires.
- **`ai-doc`** / **`template-audit`** — AI-doc authoring standard; local advisory audit of the shipped templates.
- At `env` depth also **`arch`**; at `factory` depth also `pipeline`, `dispatcher`, `harvest`, `aif-doctor`, `night-mode`, `story`, `claude-glm-executor-handoff`.

> On other harnesses (Cursor, Codex CLI, Aider, Windsurf, Roo Code) skills do **not** auto-activate — load the skill manually / read its `SKILL.md` when the topic comes up.

Add additional project skills here as you create them. Don't list a skill until its file is committed (see `references/ai-traps.md`, lesson #2).

## Sub-agents (Claude Code)

Installed in `.claude/agents/` — read the file itself for each one's protocol:

- **`review-sidecar`** — two-AI tautology review; reads tests as an external reviewer with no memory of how they were written.
- **`living-docs-auditor`** — runs `scripts/audit-ai-docs.sh` and interprets PASS/FAIL (drift + code-vs-docs).
- **`rule-researcher`** / **`rule-test-author`** — live rule research; firing test material for a generated rule.
- **`compliance-verifier`**, **`capability-reuse-auditor`**, **`memory-codification-auditor`**, **`docplan-auditor`**, **`fidelity-auditor`**, **`aif-init`** — review and audit seats; each states its own trigger.

## Test + validate commands

`package.json` owns the script list the installer wrote — run `npm run` to see it. The ones you will use daily:

```bash
npm run validate   # typecheck + lint + format:check + arch:check + audit:docs + test (parallel)
npm run test       # vitest run (unit + audit tests)
```

Mutation testing (`test:mutation:incremental`, the PR-diff gate), integration tests and coverage are in the same list.

### UI projects (react-next stack) — only if you installed Storybook / Playwright

**Not shipped by default.** `install.sh react-next` writes `playwright.config.ts`, but no `npm run` script and no `@playwright/test` dependency; Storybook is never scaffolded. Install the tooling yourself first, then add these:

```bash
npm run test-storybook             # Storybook test runner — needs @storybook/test-runner
npx playwright test                # E2E — react-next config only; needs @playwright/test
```

## Stack constraints (enforced)

- TypeScript strict + `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`; Node.js version pinned in `.nvmrc` (CI depends on it).
- ESLint 10 flat config — `eslint.config.mjs`.
- Mutation kill rate ≥70% on the PR diff (Stryker incremental); coverage thresholds per-module (`vitest.config.ts`).
- Architecture rules in `.dependency-cruiser.cjs` — fail on violation.

## Files NOT to edit without explicit reason

- `.ai-factory/RULES.md` — rules are added/changed via PR with rationale, not silently.
- `.dependency-cruiser.cjs`, `eslint.config.mjs`, `.github/workflows/ci.yml` — same.
- `scripts/audit-ai-docs.sh` — additions need a paired negative test (see `references/self-testing-docs.md`).

